To enable monitoring, add the monitor_config section to your pharos.conf file.

Example Configuration

> Note: These configuration keys require v0.16.0 or later. In earlier versions they were named enable_pamir_cetina and pamir_cetina_*. Using the wrong key names for your node version will silently disable metrics pushing without any error, so make sure the names match your node version.

``json
{
  "chain": {
    "monitor_config": {
      "storage_enable_cetina": true,
      "storage_cetina_job_name": "your_job_name",
      "storage_cetina_push_address": "your_pushgateway_address",
      "storage_cetina_push_interval": 5,
      "storage_cetina_push_port": 9091
    },
    "secret_config": {
      "domain_key_path": "../keys/domain.key",
      "stabilizing_key_path": "../keys/stabilizing.key"
    },
    "startup_config": {
      "init_config": {
        "meta_path": "../data",
        "host_ip": "127.0.0.1",
        "http_port": "18100",
        "rpc_port": "20000",
        "tcp_port": "19000",
        "ws_port": "18200"
      }
    }
  },
  "storage": {
    "mygrid_conf": {},
    "mygrid_env": {
      "storage_data_path": "../data"
    }
  }
}
`

Configuration Parameters

Add the server to your MCP client configuration:

``json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "pharos": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://mcp.pharos.xyz/mcp"
    }
  }
}
`

No API key is required.

Clients that only support stdio

Some clients cannot connect to remote MCP servers directly. Bridge to the hosted endpoint with mcp-remote:

`json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "pharos": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "mcp-remote", "https://mcp.pharos.xyz/mcp"]
    }
  }
}
`

